# SENTINEL SOC — Suricata local rules (sensor: Kali Linux, Suricata 8.0.6)
#
# Destination on the sensor: /etc/suricata/rules/local.rules
#
# PROVENANCE: this file reproduces the two custom rules from the operator's
# baseline evidence folder (phase1-suricata-baseline). Replace it with a copy of
# the real file from the sensor:
#     sudo cp /etc/suricata/rules/local.rules ./local.rules
#
# Both rules are deliberately ICMP-only probes against a public resolver used as
# an approved test target. Nothing here inspects or drops production traffic.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# sid 1000001 — DETECTION-ONLY (IDS)
# Fires on any ICMP echo and records it. `alert` never drops a packet: it is
# safe in every Suricata mode, including plain interface capture.
# Expected EVE outcome: event_type "alert", alert.action "allowed".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert icmp any any -> any any (msg:"LOCAL IDS TEST"; sid:1000001; rev:1;)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# sid 1000002 — BLOCKING (IPS)
# A `drop` rule only has an effect when the packet traverses the NFQUEUE the
# sensor is reading. Loading this rule while Suricata runs in IDS mode is
# harmless (it will simply be reported as an alert), which is why the safe
# procedure is: prove detection first, then place the packet path in the queue,
# then test, then remove the queue rule immediately.
# Expected EVE outcome: alert.action "blocked" (or an event_type "drop").
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
drop icmp any any -> 8.8.8.8 any (msg:"LOCAL IPS TEST - ICMP BLOCKED"; sid:1000002; rev:1;)
